WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.799
Pick up a TV remote.

00:00:01.799 --> 00:00:04.872
You press channel up, volume down, power.

00:00:04.872 --> 00:00:08.020
You never open the TV to see how it works.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:13.770
And if the TV is switched off, pressing channel up does nothing at all.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.360
How would you write that as a class?

00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:19.794
Welcome to Lecture 7, part three.

00:00:19.794 --> 00:00:23.905
We'll build a TV class, use two TV objects at once,

00:00:23.905 --> 00:00:32.693
and then answer a question that trips up almost everyone: what happens when you pass an object to a function?

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:38.386
Before writing a class, designers often draw it as a UML class diagram.

00:00:38.736 --> 00:00:40.904
The top box is the class name.

00:00:40.904 --> 00:00:44.733
The next box lists the data fields, with their types.

00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:52.536
A TV has a channel, from 1 to 120, a volume level, from 1 to 7, and on, which says whether it's switched on.

00:00:52.886 --> 00:00:58.079
The bottom box lists the methods, with their parameters and return types.

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:04.766
Turn on, turn off, get and set the channel and the volume, and channel and volume up and down.

00:01:05.116 --> 00:01:07.300
This diagram is a contract.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:12.556
It tells you what the class offers, without showing you any code.

00:01:13.333 --> 00:01:14.707
Here's the class.

00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:21.173
The `init` method sets up a new TV: channel 1, volume level 1, and switched off.

00:01:21.523 --> 00:01:27.003
`Turn on` and `turn off` just set `self.on` to `True`, or to `False`.

00:01:27.353 --> 00:01:32.296
`Set channel` changes the channel, but only if the TV is on,

00:01:32.296 --> 00:01:35.673
and the new channel is between 1 and 120.

00:01:36.023 --> 00:01:42.052
`Channel up` adds 1, but only if the TV is on, and the channel is below 120.

00:01:42.052 --> 00:01:45.463
The volume methods follow the same pattern.

00:01:45.813 --> 00:01:51.219
So every method that changes the TV first checks `self.on`.

00:01:51.219 --> 00:01:56.533
A TV that's off ignores the buttons, just like a real one.

00:01:57.333 --> 00:02:01.509
Now let's use it, and watch memory as we go.

00:02:01.509 --> 00:02:07.013
`TV1 = TV()` makes a TV object, with its own three fields.

00:02:07.363 --> 00:02:12.502
We turn it on, set the channel to 30, and set the volume to 3.

00:02:12.502 --> 00:02:17.723
Every call changes this object, because `self` is TV1's object.

00:02:18.073 --> 00:02:22.425
`TV2 = TV()` makes a second, separate object.

00:02:22.425 --> 00:02:28.033
We turn it on, press channel up twice, and volume up once.

00:02:28.383 --> 00:02:32.543
So TV2 is on channel 3, with volume 2.

00:02:32.893 --> 00:02:35.100
Two objects of one class.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:39.248
Each has its own channel, volume level, and on.

00:02:39.248 --> 00:02:43.573
Pressing a button on one never changes the other.

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.613
Let's trace one more.

00:02:46.613 --> 00:02:51.854
A new TV3. Set channel 50. Turn on. Channel down.

00:02:51.854 --> 00:02:53.887
What's the channel?

00:02:54.237 --> 00:02:58.983
`Set channel 50` is ignored, because the TV is still off.

00:02:58.983 --> 00:03:01.814
After `turn on`, the channel is 1.

00:03:01.814 --> 00:03:04.895
And `channel down` does nothing at 1.

00:03:04.895 --> 00:03:06.477
So the answer is 1.

00:03:07.267 --> 00:03:10.122
Here's a bug from the slides themselves.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:14.547
Their `set channel` tests `self.channel`, not the new channel.

00:03:14.897 --> 00:03:20.440
`self.channel` is the channel the TV is already on, and that's always valid.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:22.337
So the test always passes.

00:03:22.687 --> 00:03:28.007
`Set channel` of 500 gets straight through, and the channel is now 500.

00:03:28.357 --> 00:03:32.400
A setter must check its argument, the new value.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.517
The same fix applies to `set volume`.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.084
Now, objects as arguments.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:45.700
When you pass an argument to a function, the parameter is bound to the same object that the argument refers to.

00:03:46.050 --> 00:03:51.743
This function, `print_areas`, takes a circle `c`, and a number, `times`.

00:03:51.743 --> 00:03:59.810
It prints a table of areas, and after each row it adds 1 to `c.radius`, and takes 1 away from `times`.

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.431
Main makes a circle of radius 1, and sets `n` to 5.

00:04:04.431 --> 00:04:08.200
Then it passes both of them to `print_areas`.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:13.636
Inside the call, `c` points at the same Circle object as `my_circle`.

00:04:13.636 --> 00:04:17.470
And `times` points at the same 5 that `n` points at.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:26.820
Each time round the loop, `c.radius = c.radius + 1` goes through the arrow, and changes the object itself.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:30.142
But `times = times - 1` is different.

00:04:30.142 --> 00:04:34.079
It moves the local name `times` to a new integer.

00:04:34.079 --> 00:04:36.490
The caller's `n` doesn't move.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:43.017
So when the function returns, `my_circle`'s radius really is 6.

00:04:43.017 --> 00:04:44.880
But `n` is still 5.

00:04:45.666 --> 00:04:48.514
Here's the practice question from the slides.

00:04:48.514 --> 00:04:52.626
A `Count` class has one data field, `count`, with a default of 0.

00:04:52.976 --> 00:04:58.250
Main makes `c`, a `Count` object, and sets `n` to 1.

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:01.800
Then it calls `m` with `c` and `n`.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.856
Inside `m`, `c = Count(5)`, and `n = 3`.

00:05:06.206 --> 00:05:10.686
Now pause the video, and draw the names and the objects.

00:05:10.686 --> 00:05:12.926
What does the program print?

00:05:12.926 --> 00:05:15.086
Many people say count is 5.

00:05:15.636 --> 00:05:19.716
The answer is: `count` is 0, and `n` is 1.

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.540
Let's see why, in memory.

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:29.330
In main, `c` points at a `Count` object whose `count` is 0, and `n` points at 1.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.750
When `m` starts, it gets its own frame.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:39.600
Its parameters, `c` and `n`, point at the very same objects as the `c` and `n` in main.

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:45.169
Now `c = Count(5)` makes a brand new `Count` object,

00:05:45.169 --> 00:05:48.380
and moves `m`'s arrow `c` to it.

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:50.990
Main's arrow doesn't move.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:56.405
And `n = 3` moves the function's own `n` to point at 3.

00:05:56.405 --> 00:05:59.470
The `n` in main stays where it was.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.977
When `m` returns, its frame is thrown away,

00:06:02.977 --> 00:06:05.621
and the new `Count(5)` goes with it.

00:06:05.621 --> 00:06:09.660
In main, `c` still points at the object with `count` 0.

00:06:10.433 --> 00:06:14.723
Now compare a different function, `m2`.

00:06:14.723 --> 00:06:20.113
It says `c.count = 5`, instead of `c = Count(5)`.

00:06:20.463 --> 00:06:27.198
This time, the assignment goes through the arrow, and changes the object itself.

00:06:27.198 --> 00:06:30.902
And that object is also the caller's object.

00:06:30.902 --> 00:06:33.343
So the caller sees `count` 5.

00:06:33.343 --> 00:06:34.943
But `n` is still 1.

00:06:35.543 --> 00:06:37.447
So here's the pattern.

00:06:37.447 --> 00:06:46.880
Inside a function, `parameter.field = value` changes the object the caller passed in, and the caller sees it.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:51.986
`Parameter = something` only moves the function's own name.

00:06:51.986 --> 00:06:54.063
The caller sees nothing.

00:06:54.663 --> 00:06:58.260
And integers, floats, and strings can't be changed at all.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.423
So a function can never change the caller's number.

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:03.797
One last question.

00:07:03.797 --> 00:07:12.400
For our `Circle`, `circle1.set_radius(10)`, and `circle1.radius = 10`, do exactly the same thing.

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.150
So why have `set_radius` at all?

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:20.907
Because anyone can also write `circle1.radius = -5`,

00:07:20.907 --> 00:07:23.820
and the class has no way to stop it.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:25.840
Let's sum up.

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:31.950
A UML class diagram is the contract: data fields and methods, without code.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:36.538
Each object has its own fields, and its methods guard them,

00:07:36.538 --> 00:07:38.980
like the TV checking that it's on.

00:07:39.330 --> 00:07:44.183
A function that gets an object can change it, and the caller sees the change.

00:07:44.183 --> 00:07:47.650
Rebinding the parameter changes nothing for the caller.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.531
Here's one to try.

00:07:49.531 --> 00:07:53.274
Rewrite `print_areas` as `print_areas_safe`.

00:07:53.274 --> 00:07:59.568
It takes a starting radius instead of a circle, and makes its own circles,

00:07:59.568 --> 00:08:02.800
so no caller's object is ever changed.

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:08.371
In part four, we'll see how a class protects its data with private fields,

00:08:08.371 --> 00:08:10.950
and what Python really does with them.

